## 4. Apps overlay (Tahoe Apps, not classic Launchpad)
Dock icon **Apps** (`peachos-applauncher.desktop`) is **not** a GTK window. It toggles a full-screen GNOME Shell overlay owned by `macos-top-panel@local.dev`. Target look is macOS 26/27 Apps: wallpaper still visible, dark search pill, category tabs, rounded glass panel, **vertical scroll** (no page-dots).
### Files (repo → live overlay)
| Role | Repo | Live laptop |
|---|---|---|
| Overlay | `extensions/macos-top-panel@local.dev/lib/appLauncher.js` | `~/.local/share/gnome-shell/extensions/macos-top-panel@local.dev/lib/appLauncher.js` |
| CSS | same extension `stylesheet.css` (`.macos-applauncher-*`) | same live path |
| Enable wiring | `extensions/macos-top-panel@local.dev/extension.js` (`new AppLauncherOverlay()` in `enable()`) | live `extension.js` |
| Dock launcher | `apps/applauncher/peachos-applauncher` + `.desktop` | already on PATH / applications |
Copy **both** JS and CSS into the live UUID, then **log out** (ESM + stylesheet cache). Do **not** `gnome-extensions disable macos-top-panel@local.dev` to reload — that blanks the panel.
D-Bus: `org.peachos.AppLauncher` `/org/peachos/AppLauncher` method `Toggle`. The shell script calls that; if the name is missing it falls back to `org.gnome.Shell.ShowApplications` (stock GNOME grid).
### Layout (what to replicate)
```
root (chrome, monitor-sized)
  dim          — light wash rgba(8,8,12,0.22), click closes. NOT the old 0.72 black Launchpad veil
  content      — centered column, scale 0.94→1 on open
    searchBar  — 460px pill, darker glass, search icon + St.Entry + "···"
    panel      — liquid-glass plate (recipe in docs/liquid-glass-style.md), radius 28px
      cats     — All / Utilities / Productivity / Social / Photo & Video / Games /
                 Entertainment / Creativity / Information & Reading
      scroll   — 7 columns × 4 visible rows, overlay scrollbar
        gridHost (St.BoxLayout)  ← required, see crash below
          grid (St.Widget + Clutter.GridLayout)
```
Constants in `appLauncher.js`: `COLUMNS=7`, `VISIBLE_ROWS=4`, `CELL_WIDTH=118`, `CELL_HEIGHT=108`, `ICON_SIZE=62`. Dock actor is hidden by name `dashtodockContainer` while open. Escape / click dim closes. Excluded desktop ids: `peachos-applauncher.desktop`, `io.ulauncher.Ulauncher.desktop`.
Categories come from `Gio.DesktopAppInfo.get_categories()` regexes on `CATEGORY_TABS`. Search filters `get_name()` / `get_generic_name()` across the current tab. `···` resets to All and clears the query.
**No `Shell.BlurEffect`** on this overlay. Same rule as Control Center — it has crashed the session before (`docs/liquid-glass-style.md`). Glass is CSS only (translucent fill + vertical gradient + rim + inset top highlight).
### Crash that disabled the top bar (do not reintroduce)
On login after this restyle, `macos-top-panel@local.dev` went to **State: ERROR** (still listed in `enabled-extensions`, but not running). Journal:
```
TypeError: Object is of type St.Widget - cannot convert to StScrollable
AppLauncherOverlay@.../lib/appLauncher.js  (set_child)
enable@.../extension.js
```
GNOME 50 `St.ScrollView.set_child()` requires an **`St.Scrollable`**. `St.BoxLayout` is one. A bare `St.Widget` with `Clutter.GridLayout` is **not**. Putting the grid directly in the scroll view aborted `enable()` and took the **entire** top panel with it.
**Correct:**
```js
this._gridHost = new St.BoxLayout({orientation: Clutter.Orientation.VERTICAL, x_expand: true});
this._grid = new St.Widget({layout_manager: new Clutter.GridLayout(), width: COLUMNS * CELL_WIDTH});
this._gridHost.add_child(this._grid);
this._scroll.set_child(this._gridHost);  // BoxLayout, never the grid widget
```
Same pattern as Wi-Fi/Bluetooth lists (`set_child(this._networksBox)` on a `St.BoxLayout`).
`enable()` also wraps `new AppLauncherOverlay()` in **try/catch** and `logError`s so a future launcher bug cannot disable the rest of the panel. `disable()` already does `this._appLauncher?.destroy()`.
If it happens again: `gnome-extensions info macos-top-panel@local.dev` → `State: ERROR`. Then `journalctl --user -b -g 'macos-top-panel'` for the TypeError. Fix disk + live overlay, then the user can enable it (no extra logout required for a failed-to-load extension — enable loads current files).
